SYSTEM OVERVIEW
===============
The Role-based Access Control (RBAC) system manages user access to system objects in a multi-user environment based on assigned and delegated roles, security clearances, and object classification levels. It supports role hierarchy, temporary role delegation with revocation, and concurrent request processing while preserving authorization correctness, delegation and permission consistency, request liveness, and concurrency safety. Users have security clearance levels (High, Medium, Low), and requests must not exceed the user's clearance level. Administrators (a subset of High-clearance users) can approve or reject requests from users with lower clearance and delegate roles temporarily with expiration or revocation.

A prospective Emergency module is to be integrated to handle emergency situations with specific operational modes and privileges.

EXISTING-SYSTEM ASSUMPTIONS AND CONSTRAINTS
============================================
- Users are assigned distinct security clearance levels: High, Medium, or Low; these levels are uniquely named and consistently used across Clearance and Classification enums.
- Administrators have High clearance, but not all High-clearance users are administrators.
- Users hold assigned roles and may hold delegated roles subject to clearance constraints.
- Each system object has a classification level: High, Medium, or Low; classification names are unique and unified with clearance levels for consistency.
- A request’s classification level is the highest classification among its affected objects.
- Users may only submit requests with classification less than or equal to their clearance.
- Higher-clearance users may approve or reject requests from lower-clearance users.
- Requests are processed concurrently in FIFO order under Normal mode.
- Every submitted request is eventually processed (liveness).
- Users with higher clearance can temporarily delegate assigned roles to lower-clearance users if the delegate meets the role’s minimum clearance.
- Delegated roles expire after a fixed time or can be revoked anytime by administrators.
- The system preserves authorization correctness, delegation and permission consistency, liveness of request processing, and concurrency safety.

PROSPECTIVE FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS
====================================
R1: Emergency Mode Activation  
Exactly two administrators must trigger the emergency simultaneously as an atomic event while the system is in Normal mode to cause the system to enter Emergency mode.

R2: Emergency Mode Duration and Termination  
Emergency mode is active only for a fixed, explicitly defined duration, after which the system must return to Normal mode (ensuring eventual termination). Both administrators have the authority to perform a manual early termination of Emergency mode before the fixed duration elapses.

R3: Emergency Mode Operational Changes  
During Emergency mode:  
  R3.1: Request processing may bypass the normal FIFO order to prioritize processing by classification level: all High classification requests are processed concurrently first, followed by all Medium classification requests concurrently, then all Low classification requests concurrently.  
  R3.2: Users may temporarily hold roles that are mutually exclusive under normal operation. Mutual exclusivity is defined only abstractly between specific pairs of roles (without enumerating all pairs).  
  R3.3: Administrators temporarily hold special emergency privileges, modeled as atomic privilege states. Emergency privileges and credential updates are abstract atomic events, ensuring clear interaction with other operations.

R4: Post-Emergency Mode Actions  
After Emergency mode ends:  
  R4.1: Emergency privileges held by administrators must be revoked as atomic state changes.  
  R4.2: Delegated roles assigned during Emergency mode must be revoked immediately at Emergency mode termination. Delegated roles assigned before Emergency mode remain subject to normal expiration or administrator-initiated revocation.  
  R4.3: Administrators must update their credentials via an atomic credential update event.

R5: Examples and Scenarios (Illustrative)  
To reduce ambiguity, the system shall include abstract example scenarios illustrating:  
  - Activation of Emergency mode by simultaneous triggering of two administrators.  
  - Manual early termination of Emergency mode by either administrator.  
  - Revocation of delegated roles assigned during Emergency mode immediately upon mode termination.  
  - Persistence and normal handling of delegated roles assigned before Emergency mode.

KEY CONCEPTS AND ENTITIES
==========================
- User: An entity with a security clearance and assigned roles; may hold delegated roles.  
- Administrator: A user with High clearance assigned administrative privileges.  
- Security Clearance Level: One of {High, Medium, Low} with unique literal names unified with classification levels.  
- Role: A set of permissions assigned to users; may have minimum clearance requirements.  
- Delegated Role: A role temporarily assigned to a user by another user with higher clearance.  
- System Object: An entity affected by actions in requests, classified as High, Medium, or Low.  
- Request: An action submitted by a user affecting one or more system objects, with a classification equal to the highest classification of affected objects.  
- Request Processing: Handling of submitted requests, normally in FIFO order; can be concurrent and reordered by classification during Emergency mode.  
- Role Hierarchy: Relationships defining role inheritance.  
- Emergency Mode: A special operational mode triggered by two administrators simultaneously as an atomic event, active for a fixed limited time with altered request processing and role constraints, and optional manual early termination by either administrator.  
- Emergency Privileges: Temporary privileges granted to administrators during Emergency mode, modeled as atomic privilege states.  
- Mutual Exclusive Roles: Roles that cannot be held simultaneously by a user under normal operation, defined only abstractly between specific pairs of roles, but may be allowed temporarily during Emergency mode.  
- Credential Update Event: An atomic event where administrators update their credentials after Emergency mode.

ASSUMPTIONS REQUIRING CLARIFICATION
====================================
1. Two administrators must trigger the emergency simultaneously as an atomic event to activate Emergency mode.  
2. Mutual exclusivity is defined only abstractly between specific pairs of roles.  
3. Emergency privileges and credential updates are abstracted as atomic privilege state changes and atomic credential update events, respectively.  
4. Only delegated roles assigned during Emergency mode are revoked immediately at Emergency mode end; delegated roles assigned before Emergency mode remain subject to normal expiration or administrator revocation.  
5. Emergency mode duration is fixed with a parameterizable duration and includes an option for manual early termination by either administrator.  
6. During Emergency mode, all High classification requests are processed concurrently first, followed by all Medium classification requests concurrently, then all Low classification requests concurrently.

CONSTRAINTS
===========
- Clearance and Classification enums have unique literal names and are unified for consistency across the system.  
- Exactly two administrators are required to trigger the transition from Normal to Emergency mode simultaneously as an atomic event.  
- Emergency mode duration is fixed and explicitly defined; both administrators may manually terminate Emergency mode early.  
- During Emergency mode, request processing order may be bypassed to process all requests concurrently by descending classification level: High, then Medium, then Low.  
- Mutual exclusivity constraints on roles are defined only abstractly between specific pairs of roles and are relaxed temporarily during Emergency mode.  
- Emergency privileges are granted only to administrators during Emergency mode and revoked afterward as atomic privilege state changes.  
- Delegated roles assigned during Emergency mode are revoked immediately at Emergency mode end; delegated roles assigned before Emergency mode remain subject to normal expiration or administrator revocation.  
- Administrators must update credentials after Emergency mode ends via an atomic credential update event.  
- Concurrency safety constraints apply during Emergency mode, ensuring correct request processing ordering by classification without violating authorization or delegation consistency.  
- Examples and abstract scenarios illustrating Emergency mode activation, manual early termination, and delegated role revocations are provided to clarify operational behaviors.
```

